WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:03.875
<v Daniel>What if Northfield's late runs went
through Barrow instead?

00:00:05.045 --> 00:00:09.610
At 10:14 on Wednesday, Daniel Hale pitched
the best idea of his career.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:12.435
There was one problem. It wasn't his.

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:15.480
And the strange part? He had no idea.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:25.199
Two weeks after the Ostrel report, things
at Halvern were calmer.

00:00:25.379 --> 00:00:27.442
Every dashboard change was logged.

00:00:27.822 --> 00:00:30.311
And Daniel and Leah were friends again.

00:00:31.381 --> 00:00:33.586
Then Ruth announced a promotion.

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:37.131
One Senior Analyst role. Two names on the
shortlist.

00:00:37.611 --> 00:00:40.107
Bring one idea that would fix a depot.

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:43.794
For Leah, this was about more than a
title.

00:00:43.974 --> 00:00:47.374
Two weeks earlier, she'd been blamed for a
mistake that wasn't hers.

00:00:47.854 --> 00:00:50.621
A promotion would mean nobody doubted her
work again.

00:00:51.591 --> 00:00:52.828
Daniel wanted it too.

00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:55.563
Graham had put his name forward weeks ago.

00:00:55.943 --> 00:00:59.185
And Graham had made it clear he was
expecting something big.

00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:01.622
<v Leah>So. Rivals.

00:01:02.322 --> 00:01:05.352
<v Daniel>Friendly rivals. May the best idea win.

00:01:07.002 --> 00:01:12.792
<v Leah>Northfield's late runs all leave after
six. Barrow's trucks sit empty after six.

00:01:13.492 --> 00:01:16.173
<v Leah>What if the evening runs went via Barrow?

00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:18.289
<v Daniel>Huh. Maybe.

00:01:19.259 --> 00:01:21.879
Daniel heard it. He just wasn't really
listening.

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:23.359
Remember that.

00:01:25.949 --> 00:01:31.090
At 11:02, Leah wrote it down. Daniel
replied seventeen minutes later.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.753
<v Graham>Ruth wants one big idea tomorrow. Got one?

00:01:37.453 --> 00:01:39.833
<v Daniel>I've been thinking about Northfield.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:45.178
<v Graham>Good. Make it yours. Ruth remembers
whoever says it first.

00:01:46.828 --> 00:01:50.786
On Tuesday night, Daniel worked on
Northfield until midnight.

00:01:50.966 --> 00:01:53.623
He built the routes, the numbers, the
slides.

00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:56.974
By morning, the idea felt completely his.

00:01:58.624 --> 00:02:01.849
<v Daniel>What if Northfield's late runs went
through Barrow instead?

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:08.038
<v Daniel>Barrow's trucks sit empty after six. We'd
recover about forty late deliveries a week.

00:02:09.208 --> 00:02:11.900
<v Ruth>That's sharp. Whose idea was this?

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.664
<v Daniel>Mine. I've been working on it since
Monday.

00:02:19.914 --> 00:02:23.474
<v Graham>That's exactly the thinking we need from a
Senior Analyst.

00:02:24.374 --> 00:02:25.676
<v Ruth>Leah, what have you got?

00:02:26.876 --> 00:02:30.059
<v Leah>I... had something similar. I'll send it
over.

00:02:31.229 --> 00:02:33.782
<v Daniel>Leah! What did you think? Too much?

00:02:35.082 --> 00:02:36.362
<v Leah>It was a good idea.

00:02:37.062 --> 00:02:40.290
<v Daniel>Thanks. I've been thinking about it all
week.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:44.173
Leah had two obvious options.

00:02:44.353 --> 00:02:48.429
Accuse him, in front of the director, two
days before a promotion.

00:02:48.909 --> 00:02:50.845
Or say nothing, and let it go.

00:02:52.695 --> 00:02:54.259
<v Leah>You stole my idea.

00:02:55.559 --> 00:02:56.559
<v Leah>No.

00:02:57.531 --> 00:02:58.945
<v Leah>Can I show you something?

00:03:00.595 --> 00:03:03.074
Leah was sure Daniel had taken her idea.

00:03:03.654 --> 00:03:06.432
But here's the uncomfortable question. Did
he know?

00:03:07.302 --> 00:03:10.994
Psychologists call it cryptomnesia:
accidental plagiarism.

00:03:11.174 --> 00:03:16.335
In a 1989 study, people took turns naming
ideas in small groups.

00:03:16.515 --> 00:03:19.942
Later, some of them listed their partners'
ideas as their own.

00:03:20.422 --> 00:03:24.093
Memory tends to keep an idea, and quietly
lose where it came from.

00:03:25.063 --> 00:03:28.036
Our memories don't come with a label
saying who said what.

00:03:28.216 --> 00:03:32.249
The brain rebuilds the source each time we
remember, and it can get it wrong.

00:03:32.729 --> 00:03:36.549
Daniel kept the idea from Monday. He lost
the part with Leah in it.

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:40.852
Later studies found that improving someone
else's idea

00:03:41.032 --> 00:03:43.113
makes you more likely to claim it later.

00:03:43.593 --> 00:03:46.188
Daniel had spent a whole night improving
it.

00:03:47.338 --> 00:03:50.019
The second force is the egocentric bias.

00:03:50.199 --> 00:03:56.554
In a classic 1979 study, most married couples
each rated their own share of the housework

00:03:56.734 --> 00:04:01.004
higher than their partner did. Put together,
their claims came to more than the whole.

00:04:01.484 --> 00:04:05.730
Nobody was lying. We simply remember our
own effort most vividly.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.774
Studies of co-authors find the same thing.

00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:12.140
Add up everyone's claimed share, and it's
more than the whole.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:15.997
It shrinks when people stop to think about
what each person actually did.

00:04:17.447 --> 00:04:22.433
And credit doesn't land equally. In one
study, men who offered new ideas

00:04:22.613 --> 00:04:24.389
were seen as more leader-like.

00:04:24.569 --> 00:04:27.543
Women offering the same kind of ideas
didn't get that boost.

00:04:28.123 --> 00:04:31.078
None of this makes it fair. Leah still did
the noticing.

00:04:31.558 --> 00:04:33.491
But it changed what she should do next.

00:04:36.341 --> 00:04:42.093
So Leah chose a third option. Not an
accusation. A question, with evidence.

00:04:42.673 --> 00:04:46.604
She didn't need to win an argument about
memory. She had a timestamp.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:49.767
<v Leah>Can I show you something?

00:04:50.367 --> 00:04:54.210
<v Leah>On Monday, I suggested running
Northfield's evenings through Barrow.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:56.411
<v Daniel>That... was you.

00:05:00.981 --> 00:05:03.233
<v Daniel>Leah, I honestly thought it was mine.

00:05:04.233 --> 00:05:06.618
<v Leah>I believe you. That's what worries me.

00:05:07.518 --> 00:05:12.089
<v Leah>Let's take it to Ruth together. I had the
idea. You built the numbers.

00:05:13.159 --> 00:05:18.662
<v Graham>Ruth loved it. Don't overthink it. Ideas
belong to whoever makes them happen.

00:05:19.562 --> 00:05:21.509
<v Daniel>Then it belongs to both of us.

00:05:23.359 --> 00:05:27.860
<v Daniel>Ruth, I need to correct something. The
Barrow idea started with Leah, on Monday.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.253
<v Daniel>I built it out. But it was hers first.

00:05:32.423 --> 00:05:33.720
<v Ruth>Thank you for telling me.

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.803
<v Ruth>Leah, walk me through how you spotted it.

00:05:37.973 --> 00:05:43.081
<v Leah>Every late run at Northfield leaves after
six. I checked a month of logs.

00:05:43.681 --> 00:05:46.506
<v Leah>And every evening, Barrow's trucks sit
empty.

00:05:47.676 --> 00:05:49.900
<v Ruth>Noticing is the part I can't teach.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:56.491
That afternoon, Ruth sent one email.
Credit, in writing, to everyone.

00:05:58.841 --> 00:06:01.641
Ruth didn't decide the promotion that day.

00:06:02.121 --> 00:06:05.991
But she now knew who noticed things, and
who told the truth when it cost him.

00:06:07.641 --> 00:06:12.273
<v Daniel>We should run the same check on Carlton.
Its late runs leave after six too.

00:06:13.373 --> 00:06:17.127
<v Graham>Here's a thought. We check Carlton's
evening runs as well.

00:06:18.197 --> 00:06:23.718
<v Leah>Building on Daniel's point a minute ago,
yes. Carlton's next.

00:06:25.168 --> 00:06:30.689
In 2016, women on President Obama's
staff described a tactic they called amplification.

00:06:30.869 --> 00:06:35.122
When one of them made a key point, another
repeated it, and credited her by name.

00:06:35.602 --> 00:06:38.855
It's hard to take an idea that someone has
just credited out loud.

00:06:40.025 --> 00:06:42.065
Maybe Graham hadn't heard Daniel.

00:06:42.645 --> 00:06:47.292
Maybe he had. Either way, everyone in the
room now knew whose point it was.

00:06:48.942 --> 00:06:50.138
<v Daniel>Carlton next?

00:06:50.938 --> 00:06:54.548
<v Leah>Carlton next. And this time, I'm writing
it down.

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:58.912
<v Daniel>Send it to me. I'll forward it to Ruth,
with your name on it.

00:07:00.212 --> 00:07:02.361
Most stolen ideas aren't stolen.

00:07:03.141 --> 00:07:06.415
They're forgotten, by the one person who
remembers them as his own.

00:07:08.865 --> 00:07:11.275
So if someone presents your idea as
theirs:

00:07:11.655 --> 00:07:15.807
put your ideas in writing early. A
two-line message is a timestamp.

00:07:15.987 --> 00:07:19.844
Check the record before you accuse. Memory
is weak evidence.

00:07:20.024 --> 00:07:24.644
Raise it privately, with curiosity, not a
verdict. "Can I show you something?"

00:07:24.824 --> 00:07:29.032
And amplify others. Give credit out loud,
and it tends to come back.

00:07:31.282 --> 00:07:34.044
Has someone ever presented your idea as
their own?

00:07:34.824 --> 00:07:35.824
Do you think they knew?
